From bd2a732eb58edeb42b33b731378d9c8c5f272584 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dolf3131 <144650661+dolf3131@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 11:42:11 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] EXL3 decode MoE: shared expert beside the router, fused
 expert epilogues (TF_MOE_SPLIT)

A decode window's MoE block was router -> select_group -> rot_in -> grouped gate|up -> gateup_epilogue ->
grouped down -> down_epilogue on one stream, the shared expert grouped with the routed ones, and the router
(17 programs) leaving most SMs idle while nothing else could start.

- The shared expert needs only x: shared_part streams it on a side stream (rot_in, gate|up, down into its slot
  of Y) beside the router, its top-k and the routed experts, joined before the write-back.
- route_split / routed_split: the shared expert out of the groups and of rot_in (its slot keeps its pick and
  weight); gate|up's epilogue run by the last of each (expert, 128-column block)'s programs, down's (one split)
  straight from the warp sums: two launches fewer, the same arithmetic in the same order.
- grouped_kernel: an expert's n blocks are grid neighbours (its words stream together); decode windows (at most
  8 members an expert) keep an 8-row reduction buffer (half the shared memory).
TF_MOE_SPLIT=0 keeps the previous path. Extension renamed experts_v10m.

Same bits: deployed vs new on the pack's tables, rows 1-8, 96 comparisons (and picks, weights, counters left
zero); new cut-model test (verify windows 1-64 rows, logits/picks/weights, split on vs off) and the draft tests
with and without CUDA graphs pass; 201 EXL3/expert tests pass. Lab MoE block: 1 row -7.0%, 2 -6.5%, 4 -5.1%,
7 -4.2%. Live A/B (14 identical replies): +1.3..+3.8% tok/s, mean +1.75%.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
